  21. login's return shape and getSession's return shape aren't validated/typed, and there's no way to invalidate one
  session without wiping all (server.js:26-34, 36-38)
  Symptom: The public contract for a "session" is implicit ({ email, token } from login, but getSession returns whatever
  is stored, including future extra fields) and there is no logout, listSessions, or revokeAllSessions API.
  Why it matters: Beyond the security implications already covered (findings #9, #13), this is a lifecycle gap in the
  API surface: consumers can create sessions but have no supported way to manage them, forcing ad hoc workarounds (e.g.,
  directly mutating the exported module internals, which aren't even exposed).
  Fix: round out the session API with logout(id), and consider returning only the fields a client actually needs from
  login (i.e., not re-deriving email server-side from something the client already knows).

  22. Inconsistent mutability contract: listUsersByAge and findUser return live references into internal state
  (server.js:10-14, 40-46)
  Symptom: Both functions return the actual objects stored in the users array, not copies.
  Why it matters: Any caller can do findUser(email).a = 999 and silently mutate internal server state without going
  through updateAge (bypassing any validation added per finding #16). This breaks encapsulation and makes the module's
  true mutation surface impossible to reason about or audit.
  Fix: return shallow copies for read APIs:
  export function findUser(e) {
    const u = users.find((x) => x.e === e);
    return u ? { ...u } : undefined;
  }

  Style

  23. Inconsistent variable declarations: var mixed with const/let (server.js:11, 41)
  Symptom: findUser and listUsersByAge still use for (var i = ...) while the rest of the new code uses const/let.
  Why it matters: var is function-scoped (not block-scoped), which is a common source of subtle closure bugs, and mixing
  styles within one file signals the change wasn't fully cleaned up/reviewed for consistency, which erodes confidence
  in the rest of the diff.
  Fix: use for...of with Array.prototype methods (also resolves findings #1 and #5), or at minimum switch to let.

  24. Verbose object construction instead of ES6 shorthand (server.js:6)
  Symptom: { n: n, e: e, a: a, created: Date.now() } repeats each identifier instead of using property shorthand.
  Why it matters: Minor, but it's needless noise; shorthand is the established convention in modern JS and makes
  diffs/reviews faster to read.
  Fix: { n, e, a, created: Date.now() }.

  25. No JSDoc or type annotations on any exported function (server.js: entire file)
  Symptom: None of addUser, findUser, login, etc. document parameter types, return types, or failure modes (throws vs.
  returns null/undefined).
  Why it matters: This is a small, security-sensitive module (auth, session tokens) with an all-exported public surface;
  consumers integrating with it have to read the source to learn behavior like "returns undefined if not found" vs.
  "throws," which is exactly the kind of ambiguity that produced findings #1–#3.
  Fix: add JSDoc at minimum:
  /**
   * @param {string} email
   * @returns {{n:string,e:string,a:number}|undefined}
   */
  export function findUser(email) { /* ... */ }

  Testability

  26. Module-level mutable singletons (users, sessions, sessionCounter) make unit tests order-dependent and hard to
  isolate (server.js:1-3)
  Symptom: There is no exported reset/teardown function. Every test that calls addUser, login, etc. permanently mutates
  shared module state for the lifetime of the process/test file.
  Why it matters: Tests can't run in isolation or in parallel without interfering with each other (e.g., a test
  asserting users.length === 1 will fail if a prior test already added users), forcing brittle ordering assumptions or
  manual state cleanup hacks in every test file that imports this module.
  Fix: expose a reset hook for tests, or better, wrap state in a factory function that tests can instantiate fresh:
    sessions.clear();
    sessionCounter = 0;
  }

  27. Date.now() and token generation aren't injectable, complicating deterministic tests (server.js:6, 29-30)
  Symptom: created: Date.now() and 'tok_' + sessionCounter are hard-coded calls with no way to control or mock them from
  a test without global monkey-patching (jest.spyOn(Date, 'now'), etc.).
  Why it matters: Testing time-sensitive behavior (e.g., a future session-expiry check, per finding #13) becomes fragile
  and coupled to global mutation of Date/crypto, rather than clean dependency injection.
  Fix: accept a clock/ID-generator as an optional parameter (or module-level, swappable dependency) so tests can supply
  deterministic fakes:
  export function addUser(n, e, a, { now = Date.now } = {}) {
    users.push({ n, e, a, created: now() });
    return users.length;
  }

  28. No test coverage accompanies this diff at all
  Symptom: The diff adds five new exported functions (login, getSession, listUsersByAge, exportAll, importAll) and
  modifies the behavior of existing ones, with zero corresponding test file changes.
  Why it matters: Given how many of the above bugs (off-by-one loop, eval RCE, predictable tokens, broken auth) are
  exactly the sort of thing a basic unit test (findUser returns undefined for a missing user; login rejects wrong
  passwords; importAll rejects non-JSON) would have caught before merge, the absence of tests is itself a significant
  gap for a diff touching authentication.
  Fix: add unit tests per function before merging, especially negative-path tests ("user not found", "wrong password",
  "malformed import payload").

  ---

  Given the presence of a remote-code-execution vector (eval in importAll), broken/non-functional authentication,
  predictable session tokens, and multiple correctness bugs that cause crashes or silent data corruption on common
  inputs, this diff should not be merged as-is.

  VERDICT: REVISE

